endscope}\pgfsys@beginscope\pgfsys@invoke{ }\pgfsys@fill@opacity{1.0}% \pgfsys@invoke{ }{{{}}{{}}{{}}{{}}{{}}{{}}{{}}\pgfsys@beginscope\pgfsys@invoke% { }\pgfsys@transformcm{1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{1.0}{15.64897pt}{12.45842pt}% \pgfsys@invoke{ }\hbox{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\definecolor[named]{pgfstrokecolor}{% rgb}{0,0,0}\pgfsys@color@gray@stroke{0}\pgfsys@color@gray@fill{0}\hbox{% \minipage[b]{368.88869pt}\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\definecolor[named]{pgfstrokecolor}% {rgb}{0,0,0}\pgfsys@color@gray@stroke{0}\pgfsys@color@gray@fill{0}% \ignorespaces\ignorespaces\par{\color[rgb]{0.8984375,0.35546875,0.16796875}% \definecolor[named]{pgfstrokecolor}{rgb}{0.8984375,0.35546875,0.16796875}{{% User Message: }}}Determine the oxidation state of sulfur in sodium sulfite (Na% 2SO3) using the oxidation state rules and justify your answer with proper % explanation.\ \par{\color[rgb]{0.07421875,0.4609375,0.73828125}\definecolor[named]{% pgfstrokecolor}{rgb}{0.07421875,0.4609375,0.73828125}{{Assistant Message: }}}% To determine the oxidation state of sulfur in sodium sulfite (Na2SO3), we will% use the oxidation state rules: \par egin{enumerate}\par\enumerate@item@The oxidation state of an atom in a % free element is zero. \par\enumerate@item@The oxidation state of a monatomic ion is equal to its % charge. \par\enumerate@item@The sum of the oxidation states of all atoms in a neutral % molecule is zero, and in an ion, it is equal to the ion's charge. \end{enumerate}\par In sodium sulfite (Na2SO3), we have two sodium (Na) atoms,% one sulfur (S) atom, and three oxygen (O) atoms. Sodium is an alkali metal, % and its oxidation state in compounds is usually $+1$. Oxygen is a non-metal, % and its oxidation state in most compounds is $-2$. \par Let's assign oxidation states to the atoms in Na2SO3: \par egin{itemize}\par\itemize@item@Sodium (Na): $+1$ (each) \par\itemize@item@Oxygen (O): $-2$ (each) \par\itemize@item@Sulfur (S): $x$ (unknown) \end{itemize}\par Now, we will apply rule 3. Since sodium sulfite is a neutral% molecule, the sum of the oxidation states of all atoms should be zero. \par$2(+1)+x+3(-2)=0$ \par Solve for x: \par$$2+x-6=0$$\par$$x-4=0$$\par$$x=4$$\par So, the oxidation state of sulfur % (S) in sodium sulfite (Na2SO3) is $+4$. \endminipage}}}\pgfsys@invoke{ }\pgfsys@endscope}\pgfsys@invoke{ }% \pgfsys@endscope \pgfsys@invoke{ }\pgfsys@endscope{}{}{}\hss}\pgfsys@discardpath\pgfsys@invoke{% }\pgfsys@endscope\hss}}\endpgfpicture}}

